WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Audit logs should capture security-relevant events such as authentication activity,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.620
authorization failures, administrative actions, sensitive data access,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.880
configuration changes, and high-risk business transactions.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.060
Poor monitoring leaves teams with records they do not review, alerts they do not trust,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.320
or telemetry that cannot be correlated across systems.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.460
Logging is only useful when it supports timely analysis and action.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.760
Delayed detection increases impact.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.780
If suspicious activity is not surfaced quickly,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.120
attackers have more time to move laterally, modify data, destroy evidence,

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.260
or extract information.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.960
Logs need integrity and retention controls.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.780
If attackers can alter or delete the records that would reveal their actions,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.280
incident response and audit support become much harder.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.260
Centralize logs from applications, infrastructure, identity systems, and cloud services.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.200
Create actionable alerts for high-risk events.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.260
Integrate monitoring with incident response playbooks and evidence retention.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.280
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include AU-2 - Audit Events;

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:13.460
AU-6 - Audit Record Review, Analysis, and Reporting; SI-4 - System Monitoring.
